body:
`fs.readFileSync` is obviously the cause of the problem as it tries to open a file when too many are already opened.

Digging a bit more, I found out that after executing `new karma.Server({...}).start()`, there is no more file handle available. This means that the very next file access will fail, be it `chokidar` or `check-node-modules`.
Karma itself is not impacted because it uses `graceful-fs` to open files.

Adding a long synchronous wait loop after the start doesn't solve the problem.
But adding a setTimeout does, e.g:

```
gulp.task('test.unit.js', ['build.js.dev'], function (done) {
  runSequence(
    '!test.unit.js/karma-server',
    function() {
      setTimeout(() => {
        watch('modules/**', [
          '!broccoli.js.dev',
          '!test.unit.js/karma-run'
        ]);
      }, 1000);
    }
  );
});
```

It is not yet clear if it comes from Karma, graceful-fs, node or Windows ...
